我们确实在意，我们请了一位顾问，每年做一份 PDF 报告。
We do actually care, and we use a consultant who creates these PDF reports once a year.

137
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:44,720
有意思。呃，那份报告你们拿来做什么？
Interesting. Uh, what do you do with a report?

138
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:49,080
我是说，很遗憾，目前我们其实没怎么根据它采取行动。
I mean, right now I don't think we really take much action on it, unfortunately.

139
00:07:50,200 --> 00:07:51,080
什么？为什么不呢？
What? Why not?

140
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:54,240
就是很难知道那些信息该怎么用。
It's just really hard to know what to do with the information.

141
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,160
那些信息太密集了，还用了一些我们看不太懂的词。
The information is really dense and uses words we don't really understand.

142
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:01,080
跟我多说说这个。
Tell me more about that.

143
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:03,560
是这样，我们请了这位顾问。
Well, we hired this consultant.

144
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:07,789
他们让我们在一个 Word 文档里填写信息，
They asked us to fill out this word DOC with information,

145
00:08:07,789 --> 00:08:11,480
但里面填进去的很多数据，呃，好像并不准确。
but much of the data that goes into it, uh, seems inaccurate.

146
00:08:11,640 --> 00:08:15,680
我就觉得我们还得再找个专家，来搞清楚这些数据该怎么用。
And I just feel like we need another expert to figure out what to do with the data.

147
00:08:16,920 --> 00:08:22,320
明白，明白。呃，但对你们公司来说，追踪碳排放到底为什么重要？
Got it. Got it. Um, but why is it important for your company at all to track carbon emission?

148
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:26,863
是这样，我们的客户一直在问我们的排放情况，我们的银行也在问，
Well, our customers have been asking about our emissions and so has our bank

149
00:08:26,863 --> 00:08:28,200
就是我们想申请新贷款的时候。
when we try to get new loans.

150
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,480
呃，我觉得还有一些法规也快出台了。
Um, and I think there's some regulations coming up, too.

151
00:08:32,330 --> 00:08:36,210
你注意到了吗，我自始至终都没有介绍产品或解决方案。
You notice here how I never introduced a product or a solution.

152
00:08:36,650 --> 00:08:40,250
我只是提问，去了解 Amy 遇到的问题。
I only ask questions to learn about the problem that Amy is having.

153
00:08:40,330 --> 00:08:41,890
我甚至都没说我打算做什么产品。
I don't even say what I'm going to build.

154
00:08:42,570 --> 00:08:45,650
你就应该这么做。这里有一些非常具体的问题，
That's how you want to do it. Here are some very specific questions

155
00:08:45,650 --> 00:08:47,330
可以用在你的访谈里。
that you can use in your interviews.

156
00:08:47,810 --> 00:08:53,302
1. 跟我说说你现在是怎么做 X 的。X 当然是指某个具体的问题、某个任务，
1. Tell me how you do X today. By X, of course, I'm referring to a specific problem, a task,

157
00:08:53,302 --> 00:08:55,959
或者你想实现的某个目标。2.
or a goal that you are trying to solve. 2.

158
00:08:55,959 --> 00:08:59,180
在做 X 这件事上，最难的是什么？3.
What is the hardest thing you are doing about X? 3.

159
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:03,600
为什么难？4. 你多久要做一次 X？5.
Why is it hard? 4. How often do you have to do X? 5.

160
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:05,820
对你们公司来说，做 X 为什么重要？
Why is it important for your company to do X?

161
00:09:05,820 --> 00:09:07,880
一定要把这个问题深挖下去。
Make sure you dive deep into this question.

162
00:09:07,920 --> 00:09:10,320
理解他们的动机在这里会非常关键。
Like understanding their motivation is going to be critical here.

163
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:13,920
6. 你现在是怎么自己解决这个问题的？
And 6, what do you do to solve this problem for yourself today?

164
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:17,360
弄清楚他们实际在做什么，非常重要。
It's really important to understand what they're doing.

165
00:09:18,280 --> 00:09:21,480
如果你能亲眼看着他们操作，那就更好了。
Now, if you can watch them do this, that's even better.

166
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:25,953
如果他们能在视频通话里共享屏幕，或者在访谈时给你看他们的电脑，
If they can screen share during the video call or show you their laptop

167
00:09:25,953 --> 00:09:30,861
让你看看他们实际是怎么拿到那份 PDF 报告的，
as you're doing the interview and show you how they actually get the PDF report,

168
00:09:30,861 --> 00:09:33,240
他们拿它做什么，它长什么样。
what they do with it, how it looks like.

169
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:37,839
那是最理想的状态。你要深入理解他们的行为，
That's the ideal state. You want to deeply understand their behavior,

170
00:09:37,839 --> 00:09:40,230
不只是他们说了什么，而是他们做了什么，
not just what they're saying but what they're doing,

171
00:09:40,230 --> 00:09:42,240
因为改变行为会很难。
because changing the behavior will be hard.

172
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:46,400
你也可以做追问，请一定学会怎么用好这些问题。
You can also ask follow up questions and please learn how to make use of these ones.

173
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:50,680
很难指望别人在一个问题的一个回答里就把一切都说清楚。
It's very hard to get someone to say everything in just one answer to one question.

174
00:09:50,960 --> 00:09:54,440
要习惯多问这样的问题，比如“你这么说是什么意思？”
Get comfortable with asking questions like, what do you mean by that?

175
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:58,720
或者“你能多说说吗？”，又或者“这对你为什么重要？”
Or can you tell me more about that or why is that important to you?

176
00:09:58,960 --> 00:10:01,731
这里有一些你不该问的问题。比如说，
Here are some questions that you should not ask. Like,

177
00:10:01,731 --> 00:10:04,440
有些做法会让访谈偏离正轨。
there are ways you can kind of derail this interview.

178
00:10:05,130 --> 00:10:06,250
“你会用我们的产品吗？”
Will you use our product?

179
00:10:06,890 --> 00:10:08,300
他们大概率会说会，
They're probably going to say yes,

180
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:10,730
但那个回答对你其实毫无价值。
but they're not going to mean anything to you in that answer.

181
00:10:11,490 --> 00:10:14,530
第二，哪些功能能让产品 X 变得更好？
Two, which features would make product X better?

182
00:10:15,410 --> 00:10:17,810
记住，用户的任务是告诉你问题所在。
Remember, their job is to tell you about the problem.

183
00:10:18,450 --> 00:10:21,410
你的任务是思考用哪些功能来解决这个问题。
Your job is to think about what features to solve that problem.

184
00:10:21,930 --> 00:10:25,530
所以让他们做你该做的事，其实帮不了你。
So asking them about your job isn't really going to help you.

185
00:10:25,690 --> 00:10:28,780
是非题对你来说也没太大帮助。
Yes and no questions, that's not really that helpful for you.

186
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:35,180
你希望他们展开讲，给你尽可能多的具体、真实的例子。
You want them to explain and give you as many concrete and real examples as possible.

187
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:38,220
所以只是简单地问是非题并不理想。
So just simply asking yes no questions is not ideal here.

188
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:43,460
第四，在你看来，更好的产品 X 会是什么样？
And 4, how would a better product X look like to you?

189
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:46,540
在你看来，更好的碳排放统计软件会是什么样？
How would a better carbon emission counting software look like to you?

190
00:10:47,460 --> 00:10:50,048
很多用户不是产品开发者，也不是软件工程师。
Many of the people aren't product developers. They're not software engineers.

191
00:10:50,048 --> 00:10:50,820
他们不是设计师。
They're not designers.

192
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:53,390
他们不是产品经理，这不是他们的工作。
They're not product managers. This is not their job.

193
00:10:53,990 --> 00:10:55,390
他们的工作是另一回事。
Their job is to do a different job.

194
00:10:56,230 --> 00:11:00,830
是你在努力理解他们工作中难在哪里。
You are the one trying to understand what is the challenging part about their job.

195
00:11:01,870 --> 00:11:06,070
最后，同时问两个问题会让他们困惑。
And finally asking two questions at the same time, it's going to confuse them.

196
00:11:06,190 --> 00:11:07,190
也会让你得到的答案变得混乱。
It's going to confuse your answers.

197
00:11:07,310 --> 00:11:11,797
当你急着想要答案时，很容易犯这个毛病，
So this is when you're eager to get, get answers, it's very easy to do this,

198
00:11:11,797 --> 00:11:13,288
但要尽量避免。
this thing, but try to avoid it.

199
00:11:13,288 --> 00:11:16,040
不过我想强调我这里提到的其中一点。
But I want to highlight one of the things I mentioned here.

200
00:11:16,280 --> 00:11:17,972
访谈中有一个很大的风险，
There's one big danger during interviews,

201
00:11:17,972 --> 00:11:20,040
那就是只关注功能，而不是问题。
and that is to focus on features instead of problems.

202
00:11:20,800 --> 00:11:22,320
你很自然会这么做。
It's very natural for you to do that.

203
00:11:23,480 --> 00:11:26,600
你的大脑在想各种解决这个问题的办法。
Your brain is thinking through all the ways you're going to solve this problem.

204
00:11:27,320 --> 00:11:30,870
但你在这次访谈里真正要做的，是深入理解
But really what you're here for during this interview is to deeply understand the

205
00:11:30,870 --> 00:11:33,480
问题，而不是想出解决方案。那是留给
problems, not actually come up with solutions. That's for.

206
00:11:34,699 --> 00:11:38,856
另一个环节的。用户通常能提出好问题，
For a separate event. So users generally have good problems,

207
00:11:38,856 --> 00:11:41,219
但给出的解决方案通常很糟。
but also generally bad solutions.

208
00:11:41,699 --> 00:11:45,149
我举两个具体例子。Gmail 早期，
So I'll give you two specific examples. So early days of Gmail,

209
00:11:45,149 --> 00:11:48,067
用户要求 Gmail 的创始人 Paul Buchheit，
users were asking Paul Buchheit, the founder of Gmail,

210
00:11:48,067 --> 00:11:50,783
能在同一个屏幕上同时看到收件箱
to view both the inbox and the actual email

211
00:11:50,783 --> 00:11:53,499
和他们正在读的那封邮件。
that they were reading on the same screen.

212
00:11:53,779 --> 00:11:57,748
他们为什么会这么要求？原因很简单，就是 Gmail 太慢了，
Now, why would they ask for that? Well, reason was simply that Gmail was too slow

213
00:11:57,748 --> 00:12:01,520
用户不想等收件箱里每封邮件都加载出来。
and people did not want to wait to load each of the emails in the inbox.

214
00:12:01,560 --> 00:12:02,800
他们想同时看到所有内容。
They want to see everything at the same time.

215
00:12:03,080 --> 00:12:07,250
同样，在 Airbnb 早期，很多房客想拿到
Similarly, in early Airbnb, a lot of guests want to have the phone numbers of the

216
00:12:07,250 --> 00:12:08,640
房东的电话号码，好打电话给他们。
host so they can call them.

217
00:12:09,400 --> 00:12:10,978
我们心想，你们为什么需要这个？
We're like, why do you need that?

218
00:12:10,978 --> 00:12:13,960
结果发现，是他们并不完全信任这个平台。
It just turned out that they didn't fully trust the platform.

219
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:16,829
就是说我们在 Airbnb 上没有展现出足够的信任感，
Like we had not displayed enough trust in Airbnb,

220
00:12:16,829 --> 00:12:20,332
所以他们需要在真正下单前先了解房东，
so they needed to feel comfortable with getting to know the host

221
00:12:20,332 --> 00:12:23,679
感到安心。其次，
before actually making the booking. Secondly,

222
00:12:23,679 --> 00:12:28,700
用户对任何额外功能几乎都没有动力说不。
users don't have incentive to say no to really any additional features.

223
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:30,930
如果你问要不要这个功能、那个功能、另一个功能，
If you ask for this feature or this feature or this feature,

224
00:12:30,930 --> 00:12:32,620
他们大概什么都会说要，对吧？
they'll probably say yes to everything, right?

225
00:12:32,860 --> 00:12:35,596
但你才是负责排优先级的人，
You, however, are in charge of the prioritizations

226
00:12:35,596 --> 00:12:38,842
而且你比他们更有动力去搞清楚
and you have a lot more incentives than they have to figure out

227
00:12:38,842 --> 00:12:41,260
哪些功能才是最重要的。
which are the most important of the features.

228
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:43,357
那我们来聊聊下一步。
So let's talk about next steps.

229
00:12:43,357 --> 00:12:46,040
假设你已经做了五到十次这样的用户访谈。
So you've done your five or 10 of these user interviews.

230
00:12:46,400 --> 00:12:49,800
你也记好了笔记。我们来聊聊接下来该怎么做。
You've written down your notes. Let's let's talk about what you do then.

231
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:52,720
第一，确保你每一次访谈都有笔记。
1, you want to make sure you have notes from all each of the interviews.

232
00:12:53,040 --> 00:12:56,956
你可以用便利贴或类似的软件来整理这些收获。
You can use sticky notes or some similar software to organize these learnings.

233
00:12:56,956 --> 00:12:58,973
你可以把它们归到不同的类别里，
You can bucket them in different buckets

234
00:12:58,973 --> 00:13:00,966
每一类对应不同的问题，然后看看
that kind of describes different problems and see

235
00:13:00,966 --> 00:13:03,054
哪一个才是真正最重要的，
which one is actually the thing that matters the most

236
00:13:03,054 --> 00:13:05,158
然后写下你的结论。
and then you want to write down your conclusions.

237
00:13:05,158 --> 00:13:06,800
你从这一切中学到了什么？
What are you learning from all of this?

238
00:13:07,070 --> 00:13:10,737
你要用所有这些信息，提出一个关于解决方案的假设，
You want to use all of this information to create a hypothesis of what the solution

239
00:13:10,737 --> 00:13:13,670
猜测这个问题的解法可能是什么。别把这个过程想得太复杂。
to this problem might be. Don't over intellectualize this process.

240
00:13:14,110 --> 00:13:16,603
其实，你只想尽快开始设计 MVP，
Really, you just want to start designing MVP as fast as you can,

241
00:13:16,603 --> 00:13:19,720
但你要确保在决定 MVP 时手里有准确的信息
but you want to make sure you have accurate information when you decide the MVP

242
00:13:19,720 --> 00:13:21,590
然后再用同一批用户去测试它
and then you want to test it with the same users.

243
00:13:21,950 --> 00:13:24,670
弄清楚你要解决的问题是否真的有价值，也很重要
It's also important to know if the problem you're solving is actually valuable.

244
00:13:25,190 --> 00:13:29,381
我的意思是，人们会看重这个问题的解决方案
What I mean by this is that people will value the solution to the problem

245
00:13:29,381 --> 00:13:33,280
而且愿意为它付钱，或者说这个问题本身，解决它是有价值的
and enough to pay for it or the problem itself. Solving the problem is valuable.

246
00:13:33,720 --> 00:13:35,920
下面是几个判断它是否有价值的好方法
So here are some good tips to determine if it is.

247
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:39,400
第一，现在有没有人为这个领域的其他解决方案付钱？
1, are people paying money for other solutions in the space today?

248
00:13:39,720 --> 00:13:42,537
那些为碳排放核算做 PDF 报告的人
Are the people that make the PDF reports for carbon emissions accounting,

249
00:13:42,537 --> 00:13:43,400
他们能拿到钱吗？
are they getting paid?

250
00:13:43,560 --> 00:13:44,642
如果他们能拿到钱
Well, if they are getting paid,

251
00:13:44,642 --> 00:13:46,640
那就是个好迹象，说明这大概是件有价值的事
that's a good sign that this is probably a valuable thing.

252
00:13:47,080 --> 00:13:49,000
人们是不是已经有了解决这个问题的办法？
Do people already have solutions to this problem?

253
00:13:49,200 --> 00:13:52,836
而且他们对现有办法很满意，哪怕在你看来很简陋，比如
They are very happy with, even though it might appear basics to you, for example,

254
00:13:52,836 --> 00:13:56,472
像 Excel 或 Google 表格，其实是很多很多创业公司的竞争对手
like Excel or Google spreadsheets is actually competitors to many, many, many,

255
00:13:56,472 --> 00:13:59,240
数以百计的创业公司，而且是相当强劲的对手
many hundreds of startups, actually quite a formidable one.

256
00:13:59,340 --> 00:14:01,570
要让别人放弃 Excel 或 Google 表格
To move someone off Excel or Google spreadsheet,

257
00:14:01,570 --> 00:14:04,780
你得让你方案的体验好上一大截
you need to make the experience of your solution dramatically better.

258
00:14:05,020 --> 00:14:08,029
他们不会每月付你几百美元，去买一个
They're not going to pay you hundreds of dollars a month for something they can

259
00:14:08,029 --> 00:14:10,020
用 Google 表格或 Excel 几乎一样轻松就能做的东西
almost as easily do in Google Spreadsheet or Excel.

260
00:14:10,180 --> 00:14:13,820
第三，你要评估卖给这类人群有多容易
And 3, you want to evaluate how easy it is to sell to this audience.

261
00:14:14,140 --> 00:14:17,100
这也是我很爱和合作的公司一起做的一件事
And this is one thing that I like to do a lot with the companies I work with.

262
00:14:17,260 --> 00:14:21,383
比如，卖给水管工或承包商，出了名地比
For example, selling to plumbers or contractors is notoriously difficult compared to

263
00:14:21,383 --> 00:14:22,400
卖给创业公司难
selling to startups.

264
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:26,711
前两类人很少更换他们的工具或软件
The first two groups just don't change their tools or the software very often,

265
00:14:26,711 --> 00:14:29,840
而创业公司则随时愿意尝试新东西
versus startups are open to trying new things all the time.

266
00:14:30,360 --> 00:14:32,457
所以就算你解决的问题很有价值
So if you are solving a problem that's valuable,

267
00:14:32,457 --> 00:14:35,604
你还是得想想，卖给这类特定人群
you still have to think about whether it's going to be easy to sell to this

268
00:14:35,604 --> 00:14:38,327
是否容易。一旦你有了 MVP 原型
particular audience. Once you have an MVP prototype,

269
00:14:38,327 --> 00:14:40,142
你可以先把它拿给用户看
you can start by showing it to users,

270
00:14:40,142 --> 00:14:43,680
哪怕它只是 InVision 之类做的设计原型
even if it's just a design prototype in envision or something like that.

271
00:14:43,800 --> 00:14:44,520
你能直接点着走一遍
You can just click through.

272
00:14:45,040 --> 00:14:46,800
我们在 Airbnb 时经常这么做
We used to do this at Airbnb all the time.

273
00:14:47,320 --> 00:14:50,379
我们会下楼到办公室里，找一些人
We'd go downstairs in our in our office and we'd find some people

274
00:14:50,379 --> 00:14:54,247
那些在等面试、或者在等咖啡之类的人
that were waiting for an interview or waiting for getting a coffee or something,

275
00:14:54,247 --> 00:14:56,944
然后把 app 的最新版本拿给他们看
and we'd just show them the latest version of, of the app.

276
00:14:56,944 --> 00:14:59,240
这会是我们手机上的一个 InVision 原型
This would be a envision prototype on our phone.

277
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:02,760
大概就是把手机递给他们，展示给他们看
It would be like hand them our phone and, and, and show it to them.

278
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:04,640
这些东西我们甚至还没做出来
This is stuff that we haven't even built yet.

279
00:15:04,920 --> 00:15:10,480
用这个方法的关键，是不要告诉他们该怎么做
The key thing, if you're doing this tactic is to not tell them what to do.

280
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:13,440
不要告诉他们该怎么做，只要看着他们自己去摆弄
Do not tell them what to do. Just watch them play around with it.

281
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:17,771
你可以给他们一个具体目标，比如在 Airbnb 上订一次房，或者
You can tell them a specific goal like try to make a booking on Airbnb or try to do

282
00:15:17,771 --> 00:15:20,675
在 Airbnb 上按日期搜索之类的
a search with dates on Airbnb or something like that,

283
00:15:20,675 --> 00:15:23,580
但不要告诉他们每个界面到底该怎么操作
but do not tell them exactly how to solve each screen.

284
00:15:23,700 --> 00:15:25,280
记住，如果这是个真实的产品
Remember, if this was a real product,

285
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:27,980
你不会站在他们旁边给他们出主意
you wouldn't be standing there next to them to give them advice.

286
00:15:28,300 --> 00:15:30,297
做这件事时，还有一个好技巧
Another good trick when you're doing this,

287
00:15:30,297 --> 00:15:33,236
无论你是在旁听，还是在录下他们的操作
either when you're listening or having them or recording them,

288
00:15:33,236 --> 00:15:35,788
就是让他们边操作边把想法说出来
is to have them speak their mind while they're doing it,

289
00:15:35,788 --> 00:15:39,208
让他们准确说出每一步在想什么
have them describe exactly what they're thinking on each of the steps.

290
00:15:39,208 --> 00:15:41,063
也许是他们正在读的那些字
Maybe the words that they're reading,

291
00:15:41,063 --> 00:15:44,062
也许这能提示你，哪些词他们看得懂
maybe that will give you hints of what words that they understand and

292
00:15:44,062 --> 00:15:48,060
哪些词看不懂，或者某个界面是什么意思、有什么用
which words they don't understand or maybe what a specific screen means or what purpose is.

293
00:15:48,340 --> 00:15:50,882
等你做完一批这样的访谈
And after you've done a bunch of these interviews

294
00:15:50,882 --> 00:15:52,928
或许还有一些 MVP 原型测试环节之后
and then maybe MVP prototype sessions,

295
00:15:52,928 --> 00:15:57,020
让这些受访者全程参与进来，是个很好的主意
it's a great idea to keep these interviewees involved throughout the process.

296
00:15:57,980 --> 00:16:01,666
很多 YC 公司常用的一个做法是，建一个 Slack
One common idea that a lot of YC companies are using is to create a Slack instance

297
00:16:01,666 --> 00:16:03,620
或者和客户建一个 WhatsApp 群
or a WhatsApp group with their customers.

298
00:16:04,340 --> 00:16:06,820
你要让他们觉得自己很特别，对吧？
Now, you want to make them feel special, right?

299
00:16:06,940 --> 00:16:10,938
你要让他们觉得自己能独家、实时地接触到那个未来的
You want to make them feel like they have exclusive active access to the future

300
00:16:10,938 --> 00:16:14,678
会改变世界的产品。所以要想想你的措辞
world changing product. So think about how you are wording or, or,

301
00:16:14,678 --> 00:16:18,510
或者你怎么告诉他们你要把他们拉进这个 WhatsApp 群。
or how you communicate this WhatsApp group that you're adding them to.

302
00:16:18,590 --> 00:16:21,710
在开发推进过程中，持续把原型展示给他们看，是个很好的做法。
It's a great idea to keep showing them your prototype as they are progressing.

303
00:16:21,910 --> 00:16:24,479
这样能给他们一种专属感，
Then kind of give them this exclusive feeling

304
00:16:24,479 --> 00:16:26,846
觉得"我是第一个看到这个产品的人"，
that I'm the first one to see this product,

305
00:16:26,846 --> 00:16:29,550
看着产品一路演进，也看着你在做。
product as it's progressing and also as you are.

306
00:16:30,730 --> 00:16:33,984
根据他们说的话做出回应，你可以上线一个新的，
Reacting to things that they're saying you can ship a new,

307
00:16:33,984 --> 00:16:36,696
一个新的界面、新的产品版本，
a new screen with a new version of the product and

308
00:16:36,696 --> 00:16:38,730
这会建立起你和他们之间的信任。
that builds trust with it with them.

309
00:16:39,210 --> 00:16:41,450
他们会知道，你对他们的反馈反应非常快。
They now know that you react to their feedback very fast.

310
00:16:41,690 --> 00:16:46,410
最后，有些人会很喜欢认识跟他们做同样事情的人。
Finally, some of them will love connecting with other people doing the same thing as they are.

311
00:16:47,130 --> 00:16:51,410
他们平时很少有这种机会，而你正是促成这件事的人。
They don't get to do this very often. And you are the one enabling that.

312
00:16:51,450 --> 00:16:55,770
如果你做完这些步骤，就能从未来的用户那里收集到正确的信息。
If you do all these steps, you will collect the right information from your future users.

313
00:16:55,930 --> 00:16:59,382
你会让他们持续参与进来，并把信息转化成……
You will keep them involved and translate information and

314
00:16:59,382 --> 00:17:03,610
这能让你把信息转化成第一个原型和 MVP。
that allow you to translate information into a first prototype and MVP.

315
00:17:04,010 --> 00:17:06,290
总结一下，这就是我今天讲的内容。
So to summarize, here's what I covered today.

316
00:17:06,530 --> 00:17:09,690
为什么最好的创始人会在公司整个生命周期里都和用户交谈。
Why the best founders talk to the users throughout the lifetime of the company.

317
00:17:10,690 --> 00:17:14,625
如何找到你的用户、如何和他们聊，问他们什么问题，
How to find your users and how to talk to them, what questions to ask them,

318
00:17:14,625 --> 00:17:19,150
不该问什么，最后，如何把其中一些结论转化成一个 MVP。
what not to ask them, and finally, how to turn some of those conclusions into an MVP.

319
00:17:19,150 --> 00:17:20,330
谢谢大家。
Thank you, everyone.
